  "directAnswer": "To determine whether an expense can be claimed as a tax deduction under Singapore tax law, it must meet the general deduction rules under Section 14(1) of the Income Tax Act 1947, which requires outgoings and expenses to be wholly and exclusively incurred in the production of income. Additionally, the expense must be revenue in nature and not specifically prohibited under Section 15(1) (such as domestic, private, or capital expenses).",
  "keyRules": [
    "Section 14(1) of the Income Tax Act 1947: Outgoings and expenses must be wholly and exclusively incurred during the period in the production of income.",
    "Section 15(1) of the Income Tax Act 1947: Prohibits deductions for domestic or private expenses, capital sums, improvements, and non-trade expenses."
  ],
  "caveats": [
    "Business purpose and any private element of the expense must be verified.",
    "It must be established whether the expenditure is capital or revenue in nature.",
    "Appropriate supporting documentation such as invoices, receipts, or records supporting the expense and business purpose must be available."
  ],
  "statuteReferences": [
